{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` measures **777 lines** against the hard 800-line module-size gate\n(`.github/module-size-limit.txt`), which CI enforces with no allowlist. The sibling story that\nprojects two new ticket state fields needs more than the 23 remaining lines.\n\n## Approach\n\nExtract the STATUS-processing cluster into a new `_processors_status.py`: `_fold_plan_review_phase`\n(`:32-38`), `_fold_claimed_session` (`:187-208`), `_fold_close_class` (`:211-225`) and\n`process_status` (`:228-328`).\n\nThe seam already exists and is closed: `process_status` is the **sole caller** of all three folds,\nat `:279,281,282` in the fork-winner branch and `:307,308,309` in the normal branch. The one function\nthat might have made the cluster leaky does not — `process_snapshot` sets `plan_review_phase` inline\nat `:705-710` rather than calling `_fold_plan_review_phase`.\n\nThe sibling `_processors_identity.py` (179 lines) is the exact precedent, and its own header states\nthe contract this follows: it imports nothing from `_processors`, so the split stays one-way, and\n`_processors` re-exports every symbol for back-compat. The re-export block at `_processors.py:15-22`\nis the pattern to mirror.\n\nThe alternative cut — the CREATE/genesis cluster (`process_create` `:40-152` plus\n`_project_legacy_creation_channel` `:155-184`, 143 lines) — is also valid but strictly weaker:\n`_project_legacy_creation_channel` has a second caller in `process_snapshot` (`:777`), so the\nextracted symbol would have to be imported back. The STATUS cluster has no such back-edge.\n\nThis is a behaviour-preserving move: no logic is rewritten.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors_status.py` (new) — the moved cluster, ~171 lines\n- `src/rebar/reducer/_processors.py` — drops to ~632 lines, plus a re-export line\n\n## Testing\n\nNo new behaviour, so the oracle is the existing suite passing unchanged. This extraction is the\ncheapest of the set — the new module's only import is `logging`, and it touches no other module-level\nname — so the risk concentrates entirely on the re-export:\n\n- Re-export assertion: `from rebar.reducer._processors import process_status` still resolves. The\n  complete consumer set is **seven** importers, all of which must stay green with **zero** edits:\n  two in production — `src/rebar/reducer/_replay.py` (multi-line import at `:19,32`, used in the\n  dispatch table at `:97`) and `src/rebar/reducer/__init__.py` (`:39`, re-exported in `__all__` at\n  `:76`) — and five test modules: `tests/unit/test_attestations_reducer.py:13,17`,\n  `tests/unit/test_plan_review_phase_reducer.py:10`, `tests/unit/test_harness_provenance.py:20`,\n  `tests/unit/test_claimed_session.py:20`, `tests/unit/test_plan_review_phase_happy.py:6`.\n  Two of these use multi-line `from ._processors import (...)` blocks and are invisible to a\n  single-line grep for `import process_status`, so the inventory must be derived with a\n  multi-line-aware search rather than a one-line pattern.\n- Replay equivalence: replaying a fixture ticket through the reducer yields byte-identical state\n  before and after the move. This is the assertion that actually proves behaviour preservation for a\n  reducer, since a status fold silently dropping would still leave a passing import.\n- One-way import: `_processors_status` must not import `_processors`, asserted directly.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `_processors.py` and `_processors_status.py` are each at or below 650 lines; checked by the\n      module-size gate with both counts recorded.\n- [ ] `process_status` still resolves from `rebar.reducer._processors` and from `rebar.reducer`;\n      checked by importing it by both paths and running all five named test modules unedited.\n- [ ] Replaying a fixture ticket yields byte-identical compiled state before and after; checked by\n      comparing the serialized state against a pre-move capture.\n- [ ] `_processors_status.py` imports nothing from `_processors`; checked by an explicit assertion on\n      the module's imports.\n- [ ] The full existing test suite passes with no test-file edits; checked by the gating test command."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786162878205613001,"uuid":"c7f40058-6e4a-4c13-9cd7-27b04ccff8ce"}